# Line endings are NORMALIZED, not left to the platform.
#
# Without this, git on Windows checks files out with CRLF by default (core.autocrlf=true),
# and that broke real tests on a real Windows machine: five source-scan tests locate a
# function body with `strings.Index(src, "\n}\n")`, which does not match "\r\n}\r\n". Three
# of them then sliced with the resulting -1 and PANICKED; the other two silently stopped
# guarding anything. A test that cannot run on a platform is not protecting that platform.
#
# Go's own toolchain is LF-only anyway — gofmt writes LF, and `gofmt -l .` (a hard CI gate)
# would flag a CRLF-formatted file — so LF is the only self-consistent choice for sources.
# The lens/prompt files matter too: they are read verbatim as model prompts, and stray \r
# would be sent to the provider.
#
# `* text=auto` lets git normalize to LF in the repository for anything it detects as text;
# the explicit lines below remove the guesswork for the types we ship.
* text=auto

*.go      text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.jsonc   text eol=lf
*.js      text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.sum     text eol=lf
*.mod     text eol=lf
*.txt     text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf
Makefile       text eol=lf

# Shell scripts and the hook shim MUST be LF: a CRLF shebang line makes the kernel look for
# an interpreter named "bash\r", which fails with a confusing "no such file or directory".
*.sh      text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts are the one exception — cmd.exe/PowerShell want CRLF.
*.bat     text eol=crlf
*.cmd     text eol=crlf
*.ps1     text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never touch, never diff as text.
*.onnx      binary
*.onnx_data binary
*.zip       binary
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.ico       binary
